WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.120
Sustainable aviation fuel often costs 3-5 times fossil jet fuel, even though the raw inputs

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:13.420
point to a far lower floor. This is First Principles Daily, episode 62. Conventional jet fuel

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.920
benefits from decades of existing refineries built for crude oil. Those facilities have

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:24.560
already amortized their capital costs and operate at enormous throughput. The delivered

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:31.020
price therefore tracks mostly the cost of the original crude plus modest processing. Sustainable

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:37.040
alternatives begin without that inherited base. Current production depends on hydro-processed

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:43.100
esters and fatty acids, units retrofitted from older biodiesel or renewable diesel lines.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.060
Those plants stay small, often limited to hundreds of thousands of gallons per day instead

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:54.660
of the tens of millions handled in petroleum refineries. Collecting the feedstock adds

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.820
further expense because used cooking oil and animal fats arrive from thousands of scattered

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:05.940
restaurants and rendering plants. Each batch must be cleaned, transported, and tested before

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:11.560
it can enter the reactor. Hydrogen usually comes from natural gas, steam reforming, rather

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:17.720
than cheaper electrolysis. And the finished fraction still requires full ASTM certification

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:23.900
that fossil kerosene receives automatically. Capital markets assign high hurdle rates to

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:29.180
these early plants which lengthens permitting and construction and raises financing charges.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:36.340
Airlines already spend 20-30% of operating costs on fuel for long haul flights, so they

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.880
limit how much premium priced, sustainable fuel they will commit to buy. The loop closes

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:48.320
on itself, limited demand keeps plants small, small plants keep unit costs high, and high

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:55.040
costs keep demand limited. Process choices made for technical proof rather than cost also persist.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:01.800
Retrofitted biodiesel hydro-treaters carry reactor pressures, catalyst beds, and distillation

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.300
setup sized for road diesel. Not the narrower jet cut, extra separation steps and recycle

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.480
loops therefore remain permanent, each carrying its own capital and labor charge that never

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:19.860
appears in the raw material ledger. Few plants and short off-take contracts also prevent spreading

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:26.700
spare parts inventories or specialized crews across enough volume to lower unit labor. Regulatory

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:32.860
reviews for every new feedstock or catalyst add calendar time that becomes capitalized interest.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:40.040
A rough magic wand estimate starts with the main inputs for the hay for route. Waste lipid material,

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:45.760
the dominant feedstock, carries a delivered commodity price well below virgin vegetable oil.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:52.040
Collected used cooking oil trades around 30-50 cents per pound before further processing.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.620
Converting that lipid to jet range hydrocarbons requires hydrogen whose cost depends on its source.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.460
Merchant hydrogen from natural gas today runs between 1 and 2 dollars per kilogram.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:12.120
The finished sustainable aviation fuel molecule contains roughly 0.12 kilograms of hydrogen per

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:19.740
kilogram of fuel. At current merchant prices that hydrogen share contributes perhaps 15-25 cents per

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:26.440
gallon. Adding the lipid portion and minor chemicals produces a combined raw material floor somewhere

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:32.400
between 80 cents and 1 dollar 20 cents per gallon before energy or capital enters the picture.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:39.280
Delivered fossil jet fuel has recently traded between roughly 2 dollars and 3 dollars per gallon

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:45.720
at major hubs. Dividing the finished price by the material floor yields an idiot index between

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:52.540
roughly 2 and 4 for the hay for route when hydrogen is sourced cheaply. The index climbs sharply

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:58.320
once the pathway shifts to power to liquid routes that rely on direct air-captured carbon dioxide.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:05.040
Captured carbon dioxide from current direct air-capture pilots carries costs reported in the hundreds

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:12.460
of dollars per ton. Synthesizing one gallon of jet fuel requires on the order of 3.2 kilograms of

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:19.120
carbon. So the carbon dioxide alone can add several dollars before electrolysis hydrogen and synthesis

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:25.860
are counted. In that pathway the raw material floor sits several times higher and the idiot index

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:32.240
stretches into double digits. The gap between either floor and today's market price lives in several

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:38.640
concrete stages. Small plant scale inflates capital recovery per gallon because the same reactor

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.660
vessels, compressors and distillation columns must be paid for with far fewer gallons.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:50.560
Fragmented feedstock logistics add trucking, storage and quality control steps that integrated oil

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:57.400
refineries largely avoid. Certification and off-take contracts carry legal and delay costs that

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:03.400
further raise the effective price. For direct air-capture routes the energy penalty of separating

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:10.460
dilute atmospheric carbon dioxide dominates long before any reactor chemistry begins. Consider the

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:18.200
arithmetic of scale more closely. A 200,000 gallon per day HEFA unit might carry capital charges of

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:24.860
40 to 60 cents per gallon simply to recover its installed cost over a 10-year life at a 12 percent

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:31.360
return. Doubling capacity to 2 million gallons per day does not double the reactor volume or the

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.900
number of distillation trays. Many pieces of equipment scale with the two-thirds power of throughput.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:43.760
So the same capital recovery can fall below 15 cents per gallon. That single change moves the

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:50.180
idiot index noticeably closer to the material floor without altering the chemistry. The same logic

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:57.180
applies to hydrogen supply. When electrolysis is sized for a single small plant, the electrolyzer stack,

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:03.320
power electronics and water treatment system sit idle whenever the sustainable aviation fuel unit

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:09.680
is down for catalyst regeneration. A purpose-built facility co-located with a multi-hundred

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:16.500
megawatt wind or solar array can run the electrolyzers at higher capacity factor. Spreading their

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:22.760
capital cost across more kilograms of hydrogen trims another 10-20 cents from the finished gallon

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:29.720
before any reactor chemistry occurs. A redesign would first target scale by building purpose-built

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:36.040
hefa or alcohol to jet facilities sized for millions of gallons per day rather than retrofitting

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:42.860
existing biodiesel units. That move requires secure multi-year feedstock contracts or vertical

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:49.480
integration into collection networks so the plant can run at high utilization. Second, it would

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:56.100
site co-located electrolysis powered by dedicated low-cost renewables so hydrogen cost falls

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:03.160
toward the electricity price floor rather than merchant reformer prices. Third, it would standardize

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:08.280
modular reactor and separation packages that can be replicated across multiple sites,

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:14.200
cutting engineering and permitting time per installation. For power to liquid routes,

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:20.580
the priority sequence shifts. Cheaper direct air capture contactors and lower temperature regeneration

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:27.640
cycles must be proven before the carbon cost drops enough to matter. Policy that prices long-term

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:33.740
off-take or supplies low-cost capital for first-mover plants can compress the financing premium that

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:40.900
currently inflates every gallon. The hard limits remain real. Waste lipid volumes are finite and already

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:47.700
competed for by road diesel and petrochemical uses. Crop-based feedstocks collide with land use

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:54.840
direct air capture routes carry unavoidable thermodynamic work to concentrate carbon dioxide from

00:07:54.840 --> 00:08:01.940
400 parts per million and that work must ultimately be supplied by low-carbon electricity whose own

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:08.840
cost curve still has distance to travel. Airlines will absorb only a modest premium before routes

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:14.700
or fleets are adjusted so any cost reduction must still land inside a narrow band above fossil

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:21.060
kerosene. One concrete objection is that larger plants simply concentrate feedstock risk.

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:28.140
If a single facility draws from a regional network of renderers and restaurants, a disease outbreak or

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:34.680
sudden export surge in animal fats can starve the reactor. The counter move is therefore not merely

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:41.760
bigger tanks but diversified intake. Parallel lines that can accept both waste lipids and when

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:49.140
economics allow sugars or alcohols from fermentation add some piping and valve cost. That added capital

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:54.140
is small compared with the revenue stability gained from keeping the plant online year-round.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:09:00.380
One principle is that the largest cost pools often hide in the interfaces between steps.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:07.340
Collection logistics, certification cues, and financing spreads matter more than any single reactor.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:13.760
Another principle is that a fuel's magic wand floor is set by the cheapest abundant source of

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:19.740
its constituent atoms and the energy required to rearrange them, not by the price tag on the first

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:25.620
plants that happen to be built. The first visible signal that someone is attempting the ladder on

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:32.640
sustainable jet fuel would be a multi-million gallon purpose-built facility backed by long-term feedstock

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:39.020
and off-take contracts at a disclosed price well below today's sustainable aviation fuel averages.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:44.380
That's first principle's daily for today. If this changed how you see what things cost,

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:50.400
share it with someone who depreciated and subscribe wherever you listen. I'm Patrick in Vancouver,

